WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:09.666
Hello, my name is Karl Deisseroth. I'm a

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:11.566
professor of bioengineering and psychiatry

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:13.932
at Stanford and the Howard Hughes Medical Institute.

00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:16.899
And today, I'd like to tell you a little bit about

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.966
optogenetics. Some of the humble origins of this

00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:23.766
method in science. Some of its applications

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.566
and some of the interesting lessons it teaches

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:28.866
us about the process of biology.

00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:32.732
Now, I'm also a psychiatrist and a lot of the

00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:35.732
inspiration for this technology came from psychiatry.

00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:39.299
And although this is an important field in medicine,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.266
it's got a long way to go. In many ways, we haven't progressed

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:47.699
too far in our basic description of the core symptoms

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.266
and their underpinnings, their causal underpinnings

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:53.899
than we had hundreds of years ago. Here

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.632
is a depiction of some of the earliest medical model

00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:00.766
type descriptions of depression and its symptom

00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:04.532
that today we call anhedonia, the inability to enjoy

00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:09.432
normally rewarding things. And John Haslam

00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:12.832
in the early 1800s described in melancholy,

00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:14.699
what we would call depression today,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.566
they therefore neglect those objects and pursuits

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:20.166
which formerly proved sources of delight and instruction.

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:23.899
And this is about as precise as we can be today about

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.799
what anhedonia really is in psychiatry.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.599
And what's a really amazing part of this story is

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.199
microbial organisms, single celled forms of life,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.832
have given us tools that in the last few years

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:42.599
have allowed us to come to a deeper understanding of

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.066
this fundamental and important medical and scientific

00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:51.766
question. And many others like it. Now this

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:54.866
also gives an opportunity to take a broader view

00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:57.666
about what is optogenetics, where does it stand

00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:02.232
in the broader context of interventions and experiments

00:02:02.233 --> 00:02:06.566
that we could do in neuroscience. And in the brain, which

00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:12.632
as we all know, is a very complicated system with many

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:16.132
interconnecting parts, we have historically had the ability

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:19.066
to use either magnetic or electrical interventions to

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:23.799
stimulate neurons. But one problem is that they cannot

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.299
discriminate individual kinds of neurons that might be

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.566
right next to each other. They all look the same to an

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:32.132
electrical or a magnetic intervention. What optogenetics

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:36.666
allows us to do is to put a particular sensitizers -- antennas

00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:40.632
of a sort, for external sources of energy and information

00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:43.932
into specific kinds of neurons. And then we can use light,

00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:48.932
in this case, if we make light sensitive antennas or

00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:52.232
transducers, and make neurons of a particular kind respond

00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:55.532
to light, and we do that to allow different kinds of ions

00:02:55.533 --> 00:02:59.499
to flow into cells and turn neurons on or off depending on the

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.132
experiment. That's the fundamental idea of optogenetics

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:06.032
and it's something people have wanted to do for a very long

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:09.532
time. The first person who seemed to have framed the

00:03:09.533 --> 00:03:14.066
possible solution to this age old question was Francis

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:19.199
Crick in 1999. He described the essence of this problem

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.899
that we needed to control, turn on or off individual

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.599
kinds of cells. And he suggested the ideal signal

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.799
would be light. He didn't have an idea how to do it

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.132
but in his usual perceptive fashion, he had some pretty

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:34.466
good instincts, I would say. And what I'd like to do here is

00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:38.032
to sort of pay homage to the many people who've

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:40.932
thought about this and done experiments, and contributed

00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:44.899
both historically and up to the present in making all of this

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:51.399
ultimately work. Now there were early efforts dating back

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.766
to 2002, a pioneer in this field, Gero Miesenboeck

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:59.499
was able to take individual components from a metazoan

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.766
eye, the multicellular organisms we all know of course,

00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:07.432
we have light sensitive cells in our retinas. And they achieved

00:04:07.433 --> 00:04:10.666
light sensitivity with a cascade of proteins, and he was able to

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:12.466
get to the point where he could bring some of those

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:16.332
individual components of that cascade together and

00:04:16.333 --> 00:04:19.166
confer light sensitivity on neurons. And the bright areas

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:23.766
of this plot are where the light was on. And there were other

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:27.366
groups who also took what you might call a multicomponent

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:30.432
approach to try to make neurons light sensitive.

00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:33.599
Now the only thing that held back these elegant methods

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.366
was in many ways their multicomponency which contributed

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:41.899
to difficulties in targeting, and they of course would be the first

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:47.866
state showed beautifully that some delay in the onset of

00:04:47.867 --> 00:04:50.866
the responsivity, the action potentials that were fired

00:04:50.867 --> 00:04:56.032
by the cells in response to the light. And what allowed

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:59.799
this field to take off was the microbial approach as I

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.132
alluded to earlier. Now these microbial organisms,

00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:06.132
they're small, they don't have a lot of space to work with

00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:08.532
and nor do they have to do as complex processing

00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:12.999
as other organisms do. And so they had the leeway

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.732
evolutionarily, it seems, to make very efficient all in one

00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:20.599
single component systems for both detecting light

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.632
and generating ion flow. And that allowed the generalizable

00:05:24.633 --> 00:05:28.232
aspect of this solution to happen. And this is a field that

00:05:28.233 --> 00:05:32.699
has its roots back, even as far back as the early 1970s.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.199
Dieter Oesterhelt and Walter Stoeckenius in 1971

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.499
identified bacterial rhodopsin and over decades

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.799
they and a thriving community of researchers were able

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.599
to sort out how this amazing protein works, how it responds

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.066
to light, how its photocycle of the individual protein allows

00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:59.099
ions to be transported across the membrane. And this

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.966
pump type class of opsins, bacteriorhodopsin being an example

00:06:02.967 --> 00:06:05.166
is followed by the discovery of halorhodopsin,

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:07.799
where instead of protons being moved, chlorine ions

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.566
are moved instead. Peter Hegemann and his colleagues

00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:14.866
beginning with some early predictive work in 1985

00:06:14.867 --> 00:06:18.332
leading to a very important paper in 2002 showed

00:06:18.333 --> 00:06:20.932
that there were also channel type microbial opsins that

00:06:20.933 --> 00:06:23.966
opened actual pores in the membrane instead of pumping

00:06:23.967 --> 00:06:28.599
one ion at a time. And then many groups, here

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.266
Feng Zhang in my laboratory was able to find a

00:06:31.267 --> 00:06:34.232
red light activated channelrhodopsin in 2008.

00:06:34.233 --> 00:06:37.732
And a great number of different groups, Feng Zhang,

00:06:37.733 --> 00:06:40.566
Peter Hegemann, our colleague Ed Boyden, Georg Nagel,

00:06:40.567 --> 00:06:42.766
Ernst Bamberg, John Spudich, and many others

00:06:42.767 --> 00:06:46.332
have been identifying amazing, amazingly diverse

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:50.866
microbial opsins with different properties from across

00:06:50.867 --> 00:06:55.166
the phylogenetic spectrum. Now, what is striking as

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:57.666
you'll notice from this, is how long we've known about

00:06:57.667 --> 00:07:01.632
these and this happens to be my biochemistry textbook

00:07:01.633 --> 00:07:04.766
The beautiful book written by Lubert Stryer in 1988,

00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:07.566
this was the version that I used, and you can see this is

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:11.832
textbook type knowledge. Descriptions of the photocycle

00:07:11.833 --> 00:07:16.266
of bacteriorhodopsin, for example. Part of the foundational

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:20.232
knowledge of biochemists and neurobiologists. Now there

00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:23.899
were people who did try to put these microbial

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.132
opsins in different settings, in fact, successfully.

00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:29.932
This is a very interesting paper from 1994, where

00:07:29.933 --> 00:07:34.666
the bacteriorhodopsin was indeed transferred along with

00:07:34.667 --> 00:07:40.466
light sensitive flow of ions into a eukaryotic organism.

00:07:40.467 --> 00:07:47.799
But nobody had yet tried to actually create a tool for

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:51.566
neurobiology, to turn on or turn off individual kinds of

00:07:51.567 --> 00:07:55.399
cells. Neurons are complicated, they're vulnerable,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:58.032
they're sensitive, they're different but in principle,

00:07:58.033 --> 00:08:00.899
it was an experiment that could be done. And there were

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:06.099
many people trying to do it. Now the path to this

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:11.099
in my own lab started very near to the time when I

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:15.332
had started setting up my independent laboratory.

00:08:15.333 --> 00:08:21.066
And I had a very humble approach, where as you can see

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:24.466
I used very simple tallies to assess how the experiments

00:08:24.467 --> 00:08:28.199
were working, and it's interesting to look back on the

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.566
simplicity of this early experiment. But what I was doing

00:08:30.567 --> 00:08:36.866
here, I had a broader view in the lab to try different kinds

00:08:36.867 --> 00:08:40.132
of proteins to see if we could turn neurons on or off

00:08:40.133 --> 00:08:44.799
with different kinds of stimuli. I had acquired the channel

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.832
rhodopsin gene, I'd written to a group in Germany

00:08:46.833 --> 00:08:49.466
that had worked on the initial clone from Georg Nagel

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:51.899
and he sent me the clone right away, which was very

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:54.666
generous of him. I was also looking at different kinds of

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:58.532
potassium channels, to see if we could turn on or off,

00:08:58.533 --> 00:09:00.332
constitutively active or dominant negative potassium

00:09:00.333 --> 00:09:04.599
channels. And I was putting all of these into separate groups of

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.266
neurons in culture to see if I could get control over their

00:09:09.267 --> 00:09:13.132
activity level. And I was using a very simple straightforward

00:09:13.133 --> 00:09:16.932
read out of membrane depolarization, the phosphorylation

00:09:16.933 --> 00:09:19.466
of a transcription factor called Creb, which I had done

00:09:19.467 --> 00:09:24.432
a lot of work on previously. And the amazing result was that

00:09:24.433 --> 00:09:27.532
in these neurons, you can see by the red staining of the nucleus

00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:30.466
of the cell in a stimulated compared to an unstimulated

00:09:30.467 --> 00:09:33.832
neuron. Both of these were expressing the channel

00:09:33.833 --> 00:09:36.632
rhodopsin coupled to a yellow fluorescent protein,

00:09:36.633 --> 00:09:38.466
so I could see a number of things here. I could see

00:09:38.467 --> 00:09:41.066
that the gene that I'd put in was being expressed,

00:09:41.067 --> 00:09:44.066
it was being transported to the membrane, that the

00:09:44.067 --> 00:09:46.399
neuron was healthy, it hadn't exploded as a result of

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:49.166
putting this high levels of this membrane protein into

00:09:49.167 --> 00:09:54.199
the cell. Which can happen to be sure. And moreover

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:59.599
that the light activated, the light treated neuron revealed

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.966
that it had been stimulated. That its membrane had been

00:10:02.967 --> 00:10:06.932
depolarized and calcium flux had occurred. And this

00:10:06.933 --> 00:10:10.232
biochemical readout was telling us that this was happening.

00:10:10.233 --> 00:10:13.166
And in neurons that were expression a lot of this channel

00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:16.566
rhodopsin, I could see a big difference in the number

00:10:16.567 --> 00:10:21.232
of cells that were reporting on this depolarization

00:10:21.233 --> 00:10:24.332
event, and overall, this was statistically significant.

00:10:24.333 --> 00:10:28.799
And although this was a very simple, cheap, humble

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.032
experiment, this showed us that it was possible.

00:10:32.033 --> 00:10:35.966
And this was in early July of 2004, but things went

00:10:35.967 --> 00:10:38.866
very quickly after that. This is what our small

00:10:38.867 --> 00:10:42.232
group looked like at the time, you can see the two grad

00:10:42.233 --> 00:10:45.466
students, Feng Zhang and Ed Boyden, now at MIT.

00:10:45.467 --> 00:10:48.199
Feng Zhang now also very well known for his work on

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:53.199
CRISPR-Cas9 systems. But we knew that we had to do

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:56.732
a lot more than just show some neurons getting stimulated,

00:10:56.733 --> 00:10:58.832
we had to show that this was unique, that it was

00:10:58.833 --> 00:11:01.866
a potentially generalizable or versatile, that it could

00:11:01.867 --> 00:11:05.132
potentially be applied in broader systems. And there was

00:11:05.133 --> 00:11:09.532
rightly so a lot of skepticism at the time. What the students

00:11:09.533 --> 00:11:13.299
did was some really brilliant and creative work. Feng Zhang

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:16.566
designed the viral systems that would allow us to put

00:11:16.567 --> 00:11:19.732
the channel rhodopsin in a very versatile way into different

00:11:19.733 --> 00:11:23.399
brain regions in vivo, and the fact that it's just a single gene

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.399
allowed us to be confident that this would work well.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.866
Ed did a number of beautiful recordings, showing how fast

00:11:29.867 --> 00:11:32.132
the process could be, which was another important feature

00:11:32.133 --> 00:11:35.666
of the system. Both its potential generalizability and its

00:11:35.667 --> 00:11:39.966
speed. And there were many other groups working on this

00:11:39.967 --> 00:11:45.132
at the time, Stefan Herlitze, Zhuo-Hua Pan, Yawo's group

00:11:45.133 --> 00:11:49.366
in Japan. But we were able to put the different pieces

00:11:49.367 --> 00:11:52.532
together in time to get the paper out, and it was published

00:11:52.533 --> 00:11:56.066
together with two students, Ed and Feng, as well as Georg

00:11:56.067 --> 00:12:01.766
Nagel and Ernst Bamberg. And this, though, was, I

00:12:01.767 --> 00:12:04.499
wouldn't even say this was yet a fully optogenetics because

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:07.766
there was a great deal more work that needed to happen.

00:12:07.767 --> 00:12:11.032
It took about five more years of very intense effort to

00:12:11.033 --> 00:12:14.166
show that we had actually created something that was

00:12:14.167 --> 00:12:17.399
generalizable, useful, and broadly applicable, which was

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:21.499
the key goal. So optogenetics didn't start overnight,

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:27.032
experiments were humble and simple, but it took a number

00:12:27.033 --> 00:12:30.332
of years to actually say that we finally discovered how to

00:12:30.333 --> 00:12:32.732
make this work. Part of the problem was how do you get

00:12:32.733 --> 00:12:37.132
light into big mammalian brains. And this was an early

00:12:37.133 --> 00:12:40.666
sketch of Feng Zhang's, where he designed the fiber optic

00:12:40.667 --> 00:12:44.299
interface. And this was from 2006, but then this looks very

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:47.932
much like how we now do it today. So as you can see

00:12:47.933 --> 00:12:51.432
he's a good artist and a great scientist. And this

00:12:51.433 --> 00:12:54.399
fiber optic interface has since allowed us to do many

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:57.699
things, but I'll show you one other first experiment.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:03.732
This was two years later in 2007, Feng was working

00:13:03.733 --> 00:13:06.432
with some mice that we had made in collaboration with

00:13:06.433 --> 00:13:08.732
Guoping Feng and George Augustine, and these

00:13:08.733 --> 00:13:11.132
were mice designed to have channelrhodopsin

00:13:11.133 --> 00:13:15.099
expressed in the brain and what Feng did was he implanted

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:19.832
into this mouse, a fiber optic on the right side of its

00:13:19.833 --> 00:13:22.132
brain, which as you know would control movement

00:13:22.133 --> 00:13:25.466
toward or attention to the left side of the world. And

00:13:25.467 --> 00:13:28.466
when he turned on the light, as you can see here

00:13:28.467 --> 00:13:33.232
the animal immediately starts moving, starts turning

00:13:33.233 --> 00:13:36.399
to the left. And this was the moment I would say

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:40.266
we finally knew we had made something that was going to be

00:13:40.267 --> 00:13:44.532
broadly applicable and generalizable. And that was followed

00:13:44.533 --> 00:13:48.132
quickly by a collaborative set of experiments with

00:13:48.133 --> 00:13:51.932
Luis de Lecea, Alex Aravanis, Antoine Adamantidis,

00:13:51.933 --> 00:13:54.999
and others, where we applied this fiber optic interface

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.866
to target a deep structure in the brain, in lateral

00:13:57.867 --> 00:14:01.632
hypothalamus. Feng designed some promoters

00:14:01.633 --> 00:14:03.932
that would fit into a lentivirus and allow us to control

00:14:03.933 --> 00:14:07.532
just one kind of cell deep in the hypothalamus.

00:14:07.533 --> 00:14:11.332
And with this work, we were able to play in certain kinds

00:14:11.333 --> 00:14:14.199
of activity and show some of the neural codes that were

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:19.666
causal in sleep-wake transitions. And then, even then though

00:14:19.667 --> 00:14:23.499
this required some very specific fragment of a promoter,

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:25.899
the hypocretin promoter, which would fit into a virus.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:29.599
And there was still greatly so some skepticism as to how

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.066
broadly generalizable this would be. But between 2009

00:14:34.067 --> 00:14:38.866
and 2010, we finally were able to show that there were

00:14:38.867 --> 00:14:41.699
a broad array of different tools you could use to

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:45.266
generalizably target different kinds of cells. And by this

00:14:45.267 --> 00:14:49.766
point, I would say by 2010 or so, people were able

00:14:49.767 --> 00:14:53.599
to see that this could be applied to virtually any system.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:57.532
A good example of how that all worked was in our

00:14:57.533 --> 00:15:02.032
studies in anxiety. Now anxiety is a normal part of

00:15:02.033 --> 00:15:04.532
life, it's a healthy part of life, of course it can become

00:15:04.533 --> 00:15:07.632
excessive and it can be pathological in some cases.

00:15:07.633 --> 00:15:11.999
But what we were able to do is to use optogenetics

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.332
to play in patterns of activity or suppress patterns

00:15:14.333 --> 00:15:18.032
of activity in particular cells or projections in and around

00:15:18.033 --> 00:15:21.766
structures relating to the basolateral amygdala,

00:15:21.767 --> 00:15:24.799
and the bed nucleus of the stria terminalis.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:26.999
And the way we did some of these experiments is we

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:31.199
injected a virus, an adeno associated viral vector into

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:34.999
the structure of interest, the GNST, but then we were able

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.466
to position our fiber optics in other downstream structures.

00:15:38.467 --> 00:15:42.299
By doing so, we were able to recruit cells to find by having

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:45.132
a particular projection pattern, which as you can see

00:15:45.133 --> 00:15:47.866
is a generalizable strategy. You don't need a special

00:15:47.867 --> 00:15:50.999
kind of animal or a special promoter, you just need to know

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:53.699
your anatomy. And this works because the opsins are

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:56.766
trafficked down the axons of the cells, particularly if we

00:15:56.767 --> 00:16:00.999
help them out with little trafficking motifs that were

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.032
discovered in the lab by Viviana Gradinaru and her

00:16:04.033 --> 00:16:07.766
colleagues. And I'll show you an example of how

00:16:07.767 --> 00:16:10.066
this sort of thing works. This is an experiment,

00:16:10.067 --> 00:16:13.899
I'll play this movie, Kay Tye in the lab's work which

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:18.499
was published in 2011. This is a what's called an elevated

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:23.932
plus maze, this so-called closed arm has these elevated

00:16:23.933 --> 00:16:28.532
walls and you'll see that the animal prefers to spend its

00:16:28.533 --> 00:16:31.766
time in the closed arm of the maze. Very rarely venturing

00:16:31.767 --> 00:16:34.766
out into the open arms of the maze, which is like walking

00:16:34.767 --> 00:16:38.366
the plank for the animal. Both in our studies of anxiety

00:16:38.367 --> 00:16:41.232
related behaviors, what we found and what Kay was

00:16:41.233 --> 00:16:44.366
able to show, and we followed this up with a great deal

00:16:44.367 --> 00:16:47.799
of other work, was that there were anti-anxiety

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:50.766
pathways buried within the brain. When the blue letters

00:16:50.767 --> 00:16:53.532
come on here, you'll see when she started driving one

00:16:53.533 --> 00:16:56.432
of these, as we discovered, anti-anxiety pathways.

00:16:56.433 --> 00:16:59.499
And you'll see for the first time, the mouse is willing

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:03.032
to go out into the open arms and explore, which it

00:17:03.033 --> 00:17:04.499
never had done before. Nothing else changed

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:06.766
about the animal, the speed or anything else,

00:17:06.767 --> 00:17:09.999
it simply was willing to accept this risk. And this was

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:12.999
instantaneously reversible and this of course was only

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:16.032
one feature of anxiety, apprehension in the absence

00:17:16.033 --> 00:17:18.032
of immediate threat. But there are other features, too.

00:17:18.033 --> 00:17:21.266
Respiratory rate changes, subjective feeling,

00:17:21.267 --> 00:17:24.899
anxiety feels bad and resolution of anxiety feels good.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:27.832
And in later work, we were able to show different

00:17:27.833 --> 00:17:31.432
projections coming from the bed nucleus of the stria

00:17:31.433 --> 00:17:34.966
terminalis recruit different features of anxiety using

00:17:34.967 --> 00:17:37.966
this projection targeting approach, where we position

00:17:37.967 --> 00:17:41.466
the fiber optic in a different location than the injection

00:17:41.467 --> 00:17:45.066
of the virus. And this sort of thing allowed the

00:17:45.067 --> 00:17:47.999
generalizability of optogenetics to become clear.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:51.399
And so, in this sort of work, if you think back to

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:54.132
those very early humble experiments in culture,

00:17:54.133 --> 00:17:56.466
you can see it took a number of years to finally put

00:17:56.467 --> 00:18:00.366
all the pieces together. I'm often asked what's the

00:18:00.367 --> 00:18:03.932
potential clinical impact of optogenetics, and although

00:18:03.933 --> 00:18:06.366
I'm a psychiatrist, I run a purely basic science laboratory.

00:18:06.367 --> 00:18:10.266
I'm not working on clinical translation in my lab.

00:18:10.267 --> 00:18:12.799
But it's a natural question to ask, and other people

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:16.099
are working on it. The group of Antonello Bonci

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:19.966
found in 2013 that they could turn down cocaine seeking

00:18:19.967 --> 00:18:23.332
behavior in rats with optogenetic stimulation of the

00:18:23.333 --> 00:18:26.566
medial prefrontal cortex. A very striking result that

00:18:26.567 --> 00:18:30.532
they published in 2013. And that was then followed up

00:18:30.533 --> 00:18:35.299
with Antonello Bonci and some clinical colleagues with

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:38.399
a optogenetically guided clinical intervention.

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:41.099
They used transcranial magnetic stimulation, which can

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:44.566
be non-invasively delivered to awake human beings.

00:18:44.567 --> 00:18:47.066
Of course, human brains are a lot bigger but they have

00:18:47.067 --> 00:18:50.166
a lot of the same basic reward circuitry and what they found

00:18:50.167 --> 00:18:53.599
was guided by optogenetics, they were able to

00:18:53.600 --> 00:18:56.066
have a group of cocaine addicted human beings

00:18:56.067 --> 00:18:59.432
no longer seek cocaine. They had an experimental group and

00:18:59.433 --> 00:19:02.799
a control group, and they had a very powerful effect.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:06.466
This sort of thing shows how far we've come,

00:19:06.467 --> 00:19:11.399
that we can directly affect with a principle-guided

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:15.832
approach, these very powerful and important fundamental

00:19:15.833 --> 00:19:19.532
behaviors in human beings. And this makes, of course

00:19:19.533 --> 00:19:23.432
coming back to the initial inspiration that we have

00:19:23.433 --> 00:19:27.199
starting to go down this path, now we do understand

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:30.266
in a causal and precise fashion many of the cells

00:19:30.267 --> 00:19:34.266
and connections across the brain that do affect

00:19:34.267 --> 00:19:39.432
these very important and both basic and disease

00:19:39.433 --> 00:19:44.932
relevant behaviors. Now this, of course, had very humble

00:19:44.933 --> 00:19:48.166
beginnings, as you recall, those initial tallies in the lab

00:19:48.167 --> 00:19:50.099
notebook. And we've come a long way since then.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.066
But in a way, it's even more striking when you think

00:19:53.067 --> 00:19:56.632
how these very simple organisms themselves

00:19:56.633 --> 00:20:00.066
have created these tools that let us make this

00:20:00.067 --> 00:20:03.199
progress. There's a whole other world that has

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:06.566
happened as well, in terms of discovering how these

00:20:06.567 --> 00:20:09.332
basic microbial opsins work. We've been able

00:20:09.333 --> 00:20:12.866
to adjust the speed of the opsins. Their sensitivity to light.

00:20:12.867 --> 00:20:15.066
We've been able to engineer them to conduct

00:20:15.067 --> 00:20:17.666
different kinds of ions to turn cells on or off.

00:20:17.667 --> 00:20:20.866
Guided by getting to the crystal structure of

00:20:20.867 --> 00:20:23.932
one of these channel rhodopsins. And we can change

00:20:23.933 --> 00:20:26.366
the color that they respond to, as well.

00:20:26.367 --> 00:20:29.366
This has allowed us to do something very important

00:20:29.367 --> 00:20:34.532
which is to tune the amount and the timing of the activity

00:20:34.533 --> 00:20:38.466
that we play into precisely what's naturally occurring

00:20:38.467 --> 00:20:42.532
in the organism, even that same individual during a very

00:20:42.533 --> 00:20:47.666
well-defined behavior. And so this elegance and precision

00:20:47.667 --> 00:20:50.599
of the method is all the more striking when you think back

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:52.966
to how crude some of the early experiments were.

00:20:52.967 --> 00:20:57.099
And how long it took to get the whole system working.

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.966
But in the end, that's what makes biology fun

00:20:59.967 --> 00:21:03.299
and beautiful. And I hope to have conveyed some of that

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:06.467
to you today.

